   " monitoring report About WHO About WHO Partnerships Committees and advisory groups Collaborating centres Technical teams Organizational structure Who we are Our work Activities Initiatives General Programme of Work WHO Academy Funding Investment in WHO WHO Foundation Accountability External audit Financial statements Internal audit and investigations Programme Budget Results reports Governance Governing bodies World Health Assembly Executive Board Member States Portal Home / Publications / m / item / WHO Global Preparedness and Response to El Ni o-Related Public Health Risks and Emergencies - Strategic Preparedness and Response Plan - Select language - العربية 中文 fran ais русский espa ol portugu s WHO Global Preparedness and Response to El Ni o-Related Public Health Risks and Emergencies - Strategic Preparedness and Response Plan 2 November 2026 Technical document Download (965.2 kB) Overview The WHO Global Preparedness and Response to El Ni o-Related Public Health Risks and Emergencies Strategic Preparedness and Response Plan (SPRP) sets out WHO’s global strategy and operational framework for October 2026 September 2027. It translates WHO’s Grade 3 response into coordinated action across headquarters, Regional Offices and Country Offices, while supporting nationally led preparedness and response. The plan is structured around four strategic objectives: Detection and Monitoring; Protection and Prevention; Care and Support; and Health Leadership and Coordination , implemented through an Anticipate Mitigate Respond approach driven by local risks, triggers and operational deadlines. It prioritizes anticipatory action, continuity of essential health services, protection of vulnerable populations, climate-informed surveillance, preparedness, logistics and coordinated support to Member States. The initial resource requirement is approximately US$35 million , covering the first six-month operational period, with the plan designed to be reviewed and adapted as the El Ni o risk picture evolves. Related documents Global Public Health ",
