   "alth information systems Harmonized health facility assessment GIS centre for health Reports World Health Statistics UHC global monitoring report About WHO About WHO Partnerships Committees and advisory groups Collaborating centres Technical teams Organizational structure Who we are Our work Activities Initiatives General Programme of Work WHO Academy Funding Investment in WHO WHO Foundation Accountability External audit Financial statements Internal audit and investigations Programme Budget Results reports Governance Governing bodies World Health Assembly Executive Board Member States Portal El Niño Overview Funding Operations Partners Surveillance Research Training Home / Situations / El Ni o Overview El Ni o is a naturally occurring climate phenomenon that raises temperatures and alters rainfall patterns, making some parts of the world drier and others wetter. The 2026-27 El Ni o is the strongest on record. El Ni o can amplify health risks from extreme heat, drought, flooding and other climate-related hazards, increasing food and water insecurity, malnutrition, displacement, and the risk of waterborne and vector-borne diseases such as cholera and dengue. The greatest impacts are expected where these hazards overlap with weak health systems, conflict, displacement, existing outbreaks and other humanitarian needs. WHO is supporting countries through risk assessment and early warning, preparedness, protection of essential health services, supplies and logistics, community protection, and emergency coordination. Documents 2 November 2026 WHO Global Preparedness and Response to El Ni o-Related Public Health Risks and Emergencies... The WHO Global Preparedness and Response to El Niño-Related Public Health Risks and Emergencies – Strategic Preparedness and Response Plan (SPRP) sets... Download Read More 5 September 2026 Global Public Health Situation Analysis - El Nino 2026 This Global Public Health Situation Analysis summarizes the evolving health risks associated 